{
  "id": "ep184",
  "duration": 56.899,
  "beats": [
    {
      "start": 0.08,
      "end": 6.461,
      "text": "Soviet orbital satellites once confirmed an incoming nuclear strike with highest reliability.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Minuteman III launch (4333697).jpg",
        "credit": "U.S. Air Force photo by Jose Davila",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Minuteman_III_launch_(4333697).jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 0.0,
          "end": 2.767,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 2.767,
          "end": 6.467,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 6.461,
      "end": 15.681,
      "text": "On September 26, 1983, early-warning screens alerted duty officer Stanislav Petrov to five American missiles.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "designed card"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 6.467,
          "end": 15.667,
          "kind": "designed",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 15.681,
      "end": 20.451,
      "text": "With impact minutes away, computers insisted an attack was underway.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "designed card"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 15.667,
          "end": 20.467,
          "kind": "designed",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 20.451,
      "end": 27.796,
      "text": "Yet Petrov suspected a real American strike would be an overwhelming barrage, not five isolated launches.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Minuteman III in silo 1989.jpg",
        "credit": "Unknown author",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Minuteman_III_in_silo_1989.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 20.467,
          "end": 23.733,
          "kind": "cover",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 23.733,
          "end": 27.8,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 27.796,
      "end": 31.866,
      "text": "Ground radar showed clear skies, confirming his suspicion.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "ROTHR USNavy b.png",
        "credit": "US Navy",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ROTHR_USNavy_b.png"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 27.8,
          "end": 31.867,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 31.866,
      "end": 38.761,
      "text": "Petrov decided against reporting an attack, alerting headquarters instead that the system had malfunctioned.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "ROTHR USNavy b.png",
        "credit": "US Navy",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ROTHR_USNavy_b.png"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 31.867,
          "end": 38.767,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 38.761,
      "end": 44.111,
      "text": "Investigations revealed satellites encountered a rare solar alignment.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "CNX UPhysics 11 03 Molniya img.png",
        "credit": "OpenStax University Physics",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CNX_UPhysics_11_03_Molniya_img.png"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 38.767,
          "end": 44.1,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 44.111,
      "end": 50.906,
      "text": "Blinded by the glare, the sensors mistook sunlight reflecting off high-altitude clouds for missile launches.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "ISS031-E-121142 - View of Earth.jpg",
        "credit": "Earth Science and Remote Sensing Unit, Lyndon B. Johnson Space Center",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ISS031-E-121142_-_View_of_Earth.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 44.1,
          "end": 47.4,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 47.4,
          "end": 50.9,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 50.906,
      "end": 56.549,
      "text": "Glinting sunlight bouncing off clouds had triggered humanity's closest nuclear false alarm.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Minuteman III launch (4333697).jpg",
        "credit": "U.S. Air Force photo by Jose Davila",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Minuteman_III_launch_(4333697).jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 50.9,
          "end": 56.9,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
